# Issey Miyake Built New York Through Space

Issey Miyake's most useful US lesson for Yohji is that its community work in New York runs through physical space, artists and scarcity, and not through membership or creator programmes. Between December 2024 and May 2026 the house rebuilt its New York presence as three owned doors (Pleats Please in Nolita, Bao Bao in SoHo, a 13,000 sq ft flagship at 45 Madison Avenue), and the flagship added MADO, a rear gallery that has shown four distinct exhibitions in its first six months ([Issey Miyake US](https://us.isseymiyake.com/blogs/mado)). Every opening and the best-documented exhibition carried product sold only in New York, and campaigns used local imagery. Reach beyond the doors came from partners (Apple's iPhone Pocket, retailers such as SSENSE) and from earned attention, not from brand-run digital work. We found no brand-run creator programme, no membership or loyalty programme, no named executive on US strategy and no sales, traffic or attendance figure for any initiative, and each of those gaps comes from a limited search and does not prove absence. In Fournier and Lee's terms, the evidence supports a hub (the gallery and its artists) and a pool (shared taste, drops, partner audiences) and shows no web (member to member ties). Role labels in this report are Claude's inference unless stated. The first-pass benchmark is superseded, and several of its claims changed on a close read of the notes (see the section on what changed).

