## What the evidence shows and does not show about community and digital activity

The documented community mechanism is spatial and scarcity-based. MADO gives New York visitors a reason to return every six to nine weeks, and the opening capsules and Matsuyama product give them a reason to buy in person. The brand frames the gallery as a link to the art and design community and does not describe a customer club ([SO-IL](https://solidobjectives.com/work/issey-miyake-new-york-flagship/)). One trade writer contrasts MADO with luxury brands that use community language without permanent cultural infrastructure ([Architect Magazine](https://www.architectmagazine.com/?p=369728)), which is a favourable framing by the author and not the brand's claim. A tension sits inside the record, because Architect describes MADO as a discourse platform and not a sales space, yet the Matsuyama product was sold through the store during the show. The consistent reading is that the gallery floor is not a shop while exhibition product sells next door (inference).

