## Three takeaways for Yohji

Every claim in this section that goes beyond the cited facts is inference, and the framework used is Fournier and Lee throughout because the notes support no other.

**1. Build a standing gallery room and run it on a fixed cadence.** The evidence is that MADO delivered four exhibitions in about six months, with 6.5 to 9 week runs, short gaps and a pipeline split between the house's own lines, its architect and one outside artist ([MADO index](https://us.isseymiyake.com/blogs/mado)). Under Fournier and Lee this is a hub, with the room and its invited artists as the centre, plus a pool of design-minded visitors. The reasoning is that a room people can return to gives a community a place to form, while one-off events do not (inference). What it would take, in concrete terms, is a programmable room of roughly the size of a rear gallery inside or beside a New York store (MADO sits inside 13,000 sq ft, and no gallery size is published), a named programming lead, a pipeline of three to four artists or collaborators a year, and long lead times. The Matsuyama project ran about 14 months from his Tokyo show to the New York run, though the notes show no equivalent timing for the other shows. The risk is that the notes contain no evidence that MADO builds a community at all. They show a programme without any attendance, sales or retention data, and the room is six months old. A second risk is cost, since no budget or headcount is published. A third is that programming leaning on the house's own lines can read as brand content, not culture.

**2. Pair each New York moment with a store-only capsule and a campaign in the city's language.** The Kenmare opening had SOIL & LEAF, the Bao Bao opening had Lucent Love, the flagship had three exclusives, and Matsuyama had New York-only T-shirts and coats. Campaigns used the apple, martini and taxi, and the A-POC launch used a New York diorama map ([Issey Miyake US](https://us.isseymiyake.com/blogs/news/18492)). The role is mostly a pool, because scarcity and shared taste gather buyers who are loosely tied (inference). The reasoning is that this is the one repeatable format across every owned opening, so it carries the least risk of being an accident of one launch. What it would take is design and production capacity for a small capsule per launch, a store-only sales channel, a city-coded creative brief and a decision on when product reaches the web. SOIL & LEAF reached the website within weeks per one writer, so the scarcity window can be short and the team needs a stated policy. The risk is that exclusivity without a community to buy it reads as a souvenir, and that the notes contain no sell-through data to show the capsules worked.

**3. Plan to own the layer Issey Miyake leaves open, and borrow reach for the rest.** The record shows that Issey Miyake's US reach came from Apple, retailers, press and wearers, while its owned digital layer is a syndicated site pointing to a global Instagram account ([Issey Miyake US news](https://us.isseymiyake.com/blogs/news/18255)). No member-to-member mechanism, which Fournier and Lee call a web, was found. The inference is that this leaves open ground for a house that wants durable ties, since earned attention fades after a drop and a partner controls its own channel. What it would take is a New York client list with consent, invitation-led evenings run by named store staff, a wearer programme built through a 90-day social audit of who already posts the brand, and a partner calendar timed to fairs and retailers (the Frieze tie and the Opening Ceremony evening are the only documented precedents, and both are thin). Lead time for a first invitation list is short, but trust takes seasons (inference). The risk is the largest of the three, because it rests on a gap in the evidence. Issey Miyake may run clienteling or creator work that this limited search missed, and a web built from scratch may cost more than the pool and hub formats that the record does support.

## Seven options for the team to choose from

These are candidate New York initiatives that the Issey Miyake record suggests. The list is for the team to choose from, and nothing here ranks one above the others. Effort ratings are inference, since no source publishes cost or staffing.

1. **Rotating in-store gallery room.** Type is recurring programming and the role is hub. Effort is high, because it needs dedicated space, a programming lead and several months of lead time per show (the Matsuyama run started about 14 months before opening). It rests on MADO's four exhibitions and the cadence figures in row 1.
2. **Artist exhibition with a New York-only capsule in an existing space.** Type is collaboration and the role is hub. Effort is medium, because it reuses an existing room and existing fabric technology and runs about seven weeks, though artist and production lead time still applies. It rests on the Matsuyama project, which used existing A-POC printing and a short run.
3. **Fair-week VIP evening tied to a launch.** Type is in-store event and the role is pool. Effort is low to medium, since it needs a date, a guest list and a fair partner but no new space. It rests on the Frieze listing for the A-POC launch, which is unconfirmed (Low confidence), so the format is plausible but poorly evidenced.
4. **Short illustrator or artist installation series in an existing door.** Type is pop-up or residency and the role is pool. Effort is low to medium, because the New York leg ran only two weeks (10 to 24 July 2025) with limited merchandise and no online sale. It rests on BRAND NEW DAY, though the New York content is unknown.
5. **Retailer-specific capsule or partner launch evening.** Type is collaboration and the role is pool. Effort is medium, because a partner supplies venue and audience while the house supplies product and design. It rests on the SSENSE exclusive, the 2015 Opening Ceremony evening and the Camper capsule. An Apple-scale partner is not replicable, but capped supply through one partner door is (inference from Apple's single US store and the sell-out).
6. **Consented client list with invitation-led evenings.** Type is community mechanism and the role is web. Effort is medium, because it needs a CRM, trained associates and an events calendar. It rests on the absence of such a mechanism in Issey Miyake's record, so it is a gap-filling idea and not a copy of an existing practice.
7. **Wearer seeding and listening programme.** Type is digital and creator and the role is pool or web. Effort is low to medium, because a 90-day audit and a small seeding list need staff time and little capital. It rests on the Cultured and Nylon accounts of organic wearers and on the lack of any brand-run creator programme, so it is again inference.

